WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.076
Metacognition matters in day-to-day work because real tasks are full of assumptions.

00:00:04.076 --> 00:00:05.401
In debugging and troubleshooting,

00:00:05.401 --> 00:00:10.371
it helps you ask what you have actually verified and what you are only assuming.

00:00:10.371 --> 00:00:11.365
In code review,

00:00:11.365 --> 00:00:16.334
it helps you separate surface familiarity from real understanding of the data flow or risk.

00:00:16.334 --> 00:00:20.973
In writing and analysis, it helps you check whether the evidence supports the claim.

00:00:20.973 --> 00:00:22.961
Teams benefit when reasoning is visible.

00:00:22.961 --> 00:00:26.936
Shared assumptions are easier to improve when people say them out loud.

00:00:26.936 --> 00:00:30.249
A useful team habit is asking, what are we missing?

00:00:30.249 --> 00:00:34.888
Another is naming uncertainty clearly: this is confirmed, this is likely, this is unknown,

00:00:34.888 --> 00:00:36.876
and this is the next test.

00:00:36.876 --> 00:00:41.183
These habits improve communication because they make confidence and evidence easier to inspect.

00:00:41.183 --> 00:00:43.502
AI-assisted work makes metacognition even more important.

00:00:43.502 --> 00:00:48.140
AI tools can produce fluent output, but fluency is not the same as correctness.

00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:51.785
A user still needs to check assumptions, sources, constraints, and uncertainty.

00:00:51.785 --> 00:00:57.086
Tool fluency can create the feeling of understanding because the answer arrives quickly and sounds polished.

00:00:57.086 --> 00:00:59.405
Verified understanding requires review, application, and evidence.

00:00:59.405 --> 00:01:01.724
A practical AI workflow includes metacognitive checkpoints.

00:01:01.724 --> 00:01:04.375
What did I ask the tool to do?

00:01:04.375 --> 00:01:06.363
What assumptions did the output make?

00:01:06.363 --> 00:01:08.019
Which parts can I verify?

00:01:08.019 --> 00:01:09.344
What evidence is missing?

00:01:09.344 --> 00:01:13.320
Do I understand the result well enough to defend or modify it?

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.639
The goal is not to reject tools.

00:01:15.639 --> 00:01:20.278
The goal is to use them critically while keeping responsibility for judgment and understanding.

00:01:20.278 --> 00:01:22.928
This is also useful for delegation and review.

00:01:22.928 --> 00:01:26.241
When you hand work to another person or a tool,

00:01:26.241 --> 00:01:31.211
keep track of what standard the output must meet and how you will verify it.

00:01:31.211 --> 00:01:33.862
Metacognition keeps the owner of the task engaged.

00:01:33.862 --> 00:01:38.500
It prevents a smooth handoff from becoming a loss of accountability, context, or understanding.
